Low scintle counts ($N_{m scint} \lesssim 60$) produce spurious ACF oscillations and secondary peaks without requiring plasma lensing. The standard finite-scintle error (FSE) formula $\sigma_{
u_s} =  rac{
u_s}{2\ln 2\sqrt{N_{m d}}}$ with conventional filling factor $\eta \sim 0.1$--$0.3$ underestimates errors; empirical simulations show $\eta$ converges to $0.05$--$0.06$ (recommended $\eta = 0.055$) for large $N_{m scint}$ and breaks down at $N_{m scint} \le 25$. For FRB 20240114A at $2.2645	ext{ GHz}$, single-Lorentzian ACF averaging yields $\overline{
u}_s \simeq 17.50	ext{ MHz}$ (matching NE2025 $
u_s \propto 
u^{4.4}$ scaling from $1	ext{ GHz}$), while a double-Lorentzian model resolves two scattering screens with $\overline{
u}_{s,1} = 2.11	ext{ MHz}$ and $\overline{
u}_{s,2} = 20.60	ext{ MHz}$.
